content.thinking:
Wait — the degraded-upstream section runs *after* the console check, so its blocked requests don't pollute. The 42 console messages here are genuine `Log.entryAdded` browser errors for e-rara images failing with connection refused, collected earlier in the session (thumbnails in the results list/detail).

Improvement: In the final check, separate upstream resource-load failures (external URLs) from errors belonging to our own origin/code. Since the app deliberately degrades for unreachable images (and the value of the check is catching our own bugs like CSP violations or JS exceptions), I'll:

```js
const upstreamUnavailable = [];
const realErrors = consoleMessages.filter((m) => {
  if (m.level === 'warning' || /favicon/i.test(m.text)) return false;
  // The seed's imagery lives on the partner libraries (e-rara). When such a
  // host is down or throttling, the browser logs a resource error; the app's
  // fallback path is what matters and is asserted in section 9. Keep the
  // evidence but do not call an upstream outage a product regression.
  if (m.level === 'browser' && /^Failed to load resource: net::ERR_(CONNECTION|NAME|TIMED_OUT|INTERNET|NETWORK|ADDRESS)/.test(m.text) && /https?:\/\/(?!127\.0\.0\.1|localhost)/.test(m.text)) { upstreamUnavailable.push(m.text); return false; }
  return true;
});
check('no uncaught console errors during the whole session', realErrors.length === 0, ...);
if (upstreamUnavailable.length) console.log(`ℹ ${upstreamUnavailable.length} upstream image(s) unavailable …`);
report.upstreamUnavailable = upstreamUnavailable;
```

Hmm, also `ERR_CERT_*`? Keep the narrow list. External = URL not BASE_URL. `m.text` contains "Failed to load resource: net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED https://www.e-rara.ch/...". Check `!m.text.includes(BASE_URL)`? The URL in text is absolute; our own assets would be `http://127.0.0.1:8787/...`. So:

```js
const external = !m.text.includes(new URL(BASE_URL).origin);
```

Plan:
- Add `upstreamConsoleErrors` array to the report.
- Filter.
- Log a line.

Also: the `Network.responseReceived` handler only records responses (no response for refused). So failedRequests remains 0; fine.

Additionally, the app could avoid showing repeated broken thumbnails when the image host refuses connections: the detail panel has a fallback; the *list* thumbnails don't (many `<img>` fail silently → broken image icons?). Let me check whether result rows show broken images during the outage. In the screenshot 07-degraded (from the earlier good run) they block the image host deliberately; there's a fallback for the detail panel but maybe not for list thumbnails. During the e-rara outage, the results list would show many broken thumbnails. Could that be considered a product gap? The harness blocked images only in section 9 for a specific record's preview and checks the fallback. The list thumbnails likely land in a gray box or broken icon.

Improving the results list thumbnails' onerror fallback is results.js (maps-2). Not mine. Could add a CSS-level fix? An `onerror` handler is needed to replace with a placeholder. Out of my lane; note it.

Let me implement the harness console classification. Also record it in coordination notes as a tooling repair (I'm allowed: small, unblocking, noted).

After that, re-run just the console-relevant part? Running the whole harness again takes ~4 min but is the honest verification. e-rara will likely still be down, so with the fix the run should be 139/139 (if nothing else regresses). Let me do it.

Wait, also `Runtime.exceptionThrown` exceptions from our code stay failures — good.

Let me edit.

